{
  "mn2:0.1": "Middle Discourses 2 ",
  "mn2:0.2": "All the Defilements ",
  "mn2:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "mn2:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "mn2:1.3": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, ",
  "mn2:1.4": "“Mendicants!” ",
  "mn2:1.5": "“Venerable sir,” they replied. ",
  "mn2:1.6": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "mn2:2.1": "“Mendicants, I will teach you the explanation of the restraint of all defilements. ",
  "mn2:2.2": "Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” ",
  "mn2:2.3": "“Yes, sir,” they replied. ",
  "mn2:2.4": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "mn2:3.1": "“Mendicants, I say that the ending of defilements is for one who knows and sees, not for one who does not know or see. ",
  "mn2:3.2": "For one who knows and sees what? ",
  "mn2:3.3": "Rational application of mind and irrational application of mind. ",
  "mn2:3.4": "When you apply the mind irrationally, defilements arise, and once arisen they grow. ",
  "mn2:3.5": "When you apply the mind rationally, defilements don’t arise, and those that have already arisen are given up. ",
  "mn2:4.1": "Some defilements should be given up by seeing, some by restraint, some by using, some by enduring, some by avoiding, some by dispelling, and some by developing. ",
  "mn2:5.0": "1. Defilements Given Up by Seeing ",
  "mn2:5.1": "And what are the defilements that should be given up by seeing? ",
  "mn2:5.2": "Take an unlearned ordinary person who ha